WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
A flatworm has no lungs and no heart.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.060
A mouse has both. What changed when the body got bigger?

3
00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:16.343
Everything a body takes in must cross a surface, oxygen included.

4
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.567
Take a tiny cube of living tissue.

5
00:00:20.947 --> 00:00:24.807
Its gold faces are its surface, and its inside is its volume.

6
00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:28.027
Now make every side twice as long.

7
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.870
The big cube holds eight times as much inside.

8
00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.770
But its outer surface grows only four times.

9
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.710
So each bit of inside now has half as much surface to feed it.

10
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.370
There is a second change, and it matters more.

11
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.270
The centre of the big cube now sits twice as far from any face.

12
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.070
Oxygen must travel farther.

13
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:55.163
Oxygen gets in by diffusion.

14
00:00:56.583 --> 00:01:00.683
Each molecule wanders in random steps, as likely backward as forward.

15
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:06.137
Across five micrometres, that walk takes about a hundredth of a second.

16
00:01:07.437 --> 00:01:09.777
Now double the distance to ten micrometres.

17
00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.797
How much longer does the walk take?

18
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.650
Four times as long.

19
00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.690
Diffusion time grows with the square of the distance.

20
00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.730
Twice as far costs four times the wait, and ten times as far costs a hundred.

21
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.460
That is quick across a cell.

22
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.840
Now zoom out to one centimetre, about a finger's width.

23
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.700
The same random walk takes about fourteen hours.

24
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:47.683
So picture a solid ball of living tissue, breathing only through its gold skin.

25
00:01:49.003 --> 00:01:53.123
Oxygen soaks in from the surface, and the tissue burns it on the way in.

26
00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:59.437
Tissue holds little dissolved oxygen, about a minute's worth or less at resting

27
00:01:59.437 --> 00:02:02.757
mammal rates. So supply must keep pace.

28
00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:07.677
In this ideal model, a big enough ball runs out at its centre.

29
00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:12.147
How big can the ball get before its centre just runs dry?

30
00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:17.587
In this model, slow tissue allows a little over a millimetre of radius.

31
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:22.067
Busy tissue allows only a few tenths of a millimetre.

32
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.070
A flatworm takes another route.

33
00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.610
It grows long and wide, but stays thin.

34
00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.450
Every cell stays close to the outside, so diffusion is enough.

35
00:02:34.533 --> 00:02:37.313
A mouse is thick, and its tissue runs fast.

36
00:02:38.353 --> 00:02:39.853
So it moves fluid in bulk.

37
00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:44.533
Pumped air and pumped blood carry oxygen most of the way in.

38
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:49.477
Bulk flow brings the supply close, but it does not finish the job.

39
00:02:50.877 --> 00:02:55.637
Diffusion still carries oxygen across the last few micrometres, through thin walls.

40
00:02:56.767 --> 00:03:02.067
So, for the same shape fed only from outside, a bigger body means a longer path.

41
00:03:03.447 --> 00:03:06.487
Bodies stay thin, add inner surface, or pump fluid.

42
00:03:07.847 --> 00:03:11.287
In thick, busy animals, bulk flow does most of the carrying.
